<body>
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
	This program allows the radiometric correction of Remote
  Sensing images captured in the visible and infrared (not thermic) spectral
  regions. The radiometric correction allows to reduce the number of undesired
  artifacts that are due to the effects of the atmosphere or to the
  differential illumination which is in turn due to the time of the day, the
  location in the Earth and the relief (zones that are more illuminated than
  others, shadows, etc).
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.--> 

  <p>The program has been prepared for images Landsat-TM or MSS and SPOT-XS.
  However it can be scaled to practically any other spatial or airborne sensor
  by using files of RAD type (see description below). These files are very
  simple to write. In fact, the program is provided with RAD files for other
  sensors as examples.</p>

  <p>The model of radiometric correction which has been implemented in this
  program is based on the model proposed by Xavier Pons and Lluís
  Solé-Sugrañes in the article:</p>

  <blockquote>
    PONS, X. &amp; L. SOLÉ-SUGRAÑES (1994) &quot;A Simple Radiometric
    Correction Model to Improve Automatic Mapping of Vegetation from
    Multispectral Satellite Data&quot; Remote Sensing of Environment,
    48:191-204.
  </blockquote>This model has been successfully used in later words of several
  authors.

  <p>In the current implementation, however, several improvements such as the
  consideration of projected shadows, the use of different rescaling factors
  depending on the different spectral bands, etc., have been included.</p>

  <p>This help text only pretends to give an insight in the program use,
  without getting deeply into the discussion of the justification and
  foundations of the formulation of the algorithm. If you are interested in
  these aspects please refer to the article cited above.</p>

  <p>In essence, this model of radiometric corrections converts an image with
  values (DN) which are proportional to the radiance received by the sensor
  into an image in which values are reflectances. As it is known, radiance
  values are usually distributed in the interval [0,1] (or in [0,100] if
  expressed in percentage). However, in order to avoid the use of files of
  &quot;real&quot; type (=&quot;float&quot;), when the original data does not
  have a precision superior to 8 bits, the program writes the results in a
  file of &quot;byte&quot; type. The program allows to rescale the values to
  the interval [0,255] in the form the user finds more adequate.</p>

  <p>The model of radiometric correction takes several factors, among others,
  into account: relief (incidence angles and projected shadows), solar
  position, Earth-Sun distance in the moment of image capture, the decrease in
  radiation in its way down and up because of the atmosphere, and standard
  parameters for each channel such as atmospheric optical density,
  exoatmospheric solar irradiance and sensor calibration. The program performs
  the correction for a single spectral channel in every execution. In order to
  correct other channels you only need to execute the program again with the
  appropriate files and parameters.</p>

  <p>The program needs two auxiliary images. The first one will have the
  cosines of the incidence angle of the solar radiantion as pixel values. The
  second one will have the solar azimuth with which the image was captured,
  that is the elevation of the sun which cause the projected shadows in each
  pixel (below see the programs that allow to create these images).</p>

  <p>The atmospheric correction is based on the substraction of the lowest
  radiometric values for each channel. This value is named Kl. When
  determining Kl it is not recommended to do it 'blindly' by assigning
  the minimum value of the histogram. Instead, it is recommended to visually
  inspect the zone with the minimum values in order to determine whether it
  makes sense or not that it has such low values. For instance, if the image
  presents zones with no data (0 value) or with punctual low values because of
  a malfunctioning of the sensor, we will not assign these values to Kl. A
  visual query of these values with MiraMon (query by attributes on the lowest
  values for each channel) has proved to be very useful in the determination
  of Kl. Finally, you should remember that the value of Kl to be applied in an
  image without neither water nor hard shadows (soft relief in relation to
  solar elevation) may even be below the minimum value of the image.</p>

  <p>Although this correction model works with incidence angles inferior to 90
  degrees, it is more realistic to assume that with angles that are close to
  this value the model can not be assumed to be lambertian. Therefore, it is
  necessary to indicate the angle, in degrees, from which the correction model
  will not be applied (e.g. 73, 70, etc).</p>

  <p>On the other hand, and due to imprecisions in the Digital Elevation Model
  (DEM) from which the shadows image has been calculated, we might treat
  pixels which are shadowed as illuminated. For this reason, it is convenient
  to indicate the estimated error of the DEM (indicate 0 if unknown). This
  error must be indicated in the same units as the reference system. Sensors
  which are currently supported are:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Landsat-4 (TM) [Before august 1993]</li>

    <li>Landsat-4 (TM) [Between august 1983 and January 15th, 1984]</li>

    <li>Landsat-4 (TM) [After January 15th, 1984]</li>

    <li>Landsat-5 (TM)</li>

    <li>SPOT-1 (XS)</li>

    <li>Landsat-1 (MSS)</li>

    <li>Landsat-2 (MSS) [From June 22nd to July 15th 1975]</li>

    <li>Landsat-2 (MSS) [July 16th, 1975 and later dates]</li>

    <li>Landsat-3 (MSS) [From March 5th to May 31st 1978]</li>

    <li>Landsat-3 (MSS) [June 1st, 1978 and later dates]</li>

    <li>Landsat-4 (MSS) [Before August 22nd, 1982]</li>

    <li>Landsat-4 (MSS) [From August 26th, 1982 to March 31st, 1983]</li>

    <li>Landsat-4 (MSS) [April 1st, 1983 or later dates]</li>

    <li>Landsat-5 (MSS) [Before April 6th, 1984]</li>

    <li>Landsat-5 (MSS) [From April 6th, 1984 to November 8th, 1984]</li>

    <li>Landsat-5 (MSS) [November 8th, 1984 or later dates]</li>

    <li>Landsat-7 (ETM+) [high and low gain; NLAPS and LPGS]</li>
  </ol>Valid channels for TM: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 i 7<br/>
  Valid channels for SPOT: 1, 2 i 3<br/>
  Valid channels for MSS: 1, 2, 3 i 4

  <p>To work with a sensor not included in the above list it is necessary to
  enter the needed parameters in a text file.<br/>
  The name of this file will be indicated in the parameter 11 instead of the
  index previously given. The needed parameters are 4 for each spectral
  channel:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>solar exoatmospheric irradiance (S0, in W·m-2·um-1),</li>

    <li>atmospheric optical density in the Sun-Earth path (tau0),</li>

    <li>atmospheric optical density in the Earth-Sensor path
    (tau0_retorn),</li>

    <li>calibration factor for the sensor (to transform DN to Radiance
    expressed in W·m-2·sr-1·um-1)</li>
  </ul>These parameters must be place in a section named <font face="Courier New" size="-1">[CALIBR]</font>, under keys named:

  <ul>
    <li><font face="Courier New" size="-1">x_S0</font></li>

    <li><font face="Courier New" size="-1">x_tau0</font></li>

    <li><font face="Courier New" size="-1">x_tau0_ret</font></li>

    <li><font face="Courier New" size="-1">x_DN2Rad</font></li>
  </ul>where '<font face="Courier New" size="-1">x</font>' indicates
  the number of the spectral band.

  <p>In addition, there can be another section named <font face="Courier New" size="-1">[SENSOR]</font> with a key:<br/></p>

  <ul>
    <li><font face="Courier New" size="-1">Title (Translation note: Title
    means title)</font></li>
  </ul>and a section <font face="Courier New" size="-1">[CONFIG]</font> with
  information on the spectral configuration of the sensor, with the key
  '<font face="Courier New" size="-1">NBand</font>' which indicates
  the number of bands and keys:

  <ul>
    <li><font face="Courier New" size="-1">x_lambda_inf</font></li>

    <li><font face="Courier New" size="-1">x_lambda_sup</font></li>
  </ul>which refer to the spectral scope of each band '<font face="Courier New" size="-1">x</font>' (in micrometres).

  <p>Example of a *.RAD file:</p>
  <pre>
  [SENSOR]
  Titol=CASI Sensor. Configuration for flight dated 8-11-1994.

  [CONFIG]
  NBand=3
  1_lambda_inf=0.4973
  1_lambda_sup=0.5044
  2_lambda_inf=0.5970
  2_lambda_sup=0.6042
  3_lambda_inf=0.7085
  3_lambda_sup=0.7157

  [CALIBR]
  1_S0=1911.254
  1_tau0=0.449
  1_tau0_ret=0.284
  1_DN2Rad=0.47059
  2_S0=1745.934
  2_tau0=0.279
  2_tau0_ret=0.228
  2_DN2Rad=0.47059
  3_S0=1388.889
  3_tau0=0.234
  3_tau0_ret=0.208
  3_DN2Rad=0.47059
</pre>From application version 5, this *.RAD file format must have a section
that identifies the corresponding version:
  <pre>
  [VERSIO]
  Vers=5
</pre>Reflectance can present values between 0 and 1, although they will
usually be inferior to 0.75, and even to 0.5. As mentioned above, since the
resulting image has values ranging from 0 to 255, it is necessary to indicate
the factor needed to rescale the reflectance between 0 and 255.<br/>
  Examples:

  <blockquote>
    318.3 will cause saturation from reflectance values starting at
    0.801<br/>
    510.0 will cause saturation from reflectance values starting at
    0.500<br/>
    637.5 will cause saturation from reflectance values starting at
    0.400<br/>
  </blockquote>Staurated reflectances are assigned the value 254. Value 255 is
  reserved for pixels where the model can not give correct data. For instance,
  where incidence angles are superior to 90 degrees or to the lambertian angle
  indicated by the user or in pixels that are shadowed by other pixels or,
  rarely, where there are anomalous reflectances (greter than 1, normally due
  to an incorrect match between the images and the DEM.)

  <p>In some images, specially in MSS images processed by EURIMAGE, it is
  necesary to perform a lineal transformation to the data prior to its
  correction. This is due to the fact that the histogram of the original data
  has been stretched with aesthetic purposes. This transformation can be done
  with the parameter &quot;slope&quot;.</p>

  <p>ATENTTION: In this case, the Kl specified is supposed to be extracted
  from the images originally &quot;stretched&quot; and, thus, the program
  performs the substraction DN-Kl before applying the solicited lineal
  transformation.</p>

  <p>NOTES:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>1/ Program ASTRES allows to know the solar azimuth and elevation in
    the center of any image.</li>

    <li>2/ Program ILLUM allows to generate the image of the cosines of the
    angles of incidence.</li>

    <li>3/ Program OMBRA allows to generate the image with the illumination
    thresholds for any solar azimuth.</li>

    <li>4/ Program A_i_B allows to obtain the &quot;slope&quot; values for the
    MSS images provided by EURIMAGE.</li>

    <li>5/ If a value Kl which is to low is given the effect of illumination
    will be inverted and the mountain slopes will be seen more illuminated
    than originally.</li>
  </ul>
